          "name": "Which IT office handles notices in Mumbai?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "CPC Bengaluru issues Section 143(1) intimations and 139(9) defective return notices. Jurisdictional AOs in Mumbai (based on PAN zone) handle 143(2) scrutiny and 148 reassessment. Commissioner (International Tax) Mumbai handles transfer pricing cases. ITAT Mumbai handles appeals. All responses submitted via e-Proceedings on incometax.gov.in. Patron handles responses for all Mumbai jurisdictions."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What should I do first when I receive an IT notice?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Do not panic. Most notices are routine. First: identify the section number and deadline. Second: download the notice from the e-filing portal. Third: understand what is being asked (correction, information, payment, or appearance). Fourth: gather relevant documents (ITR, Form 16, 26AS, AIS). Fifth: respond within the deadline. For complex notices (143(2) scrutiny, 148 reassessment), engage a CA immediately. Patron provides same-day analysis for Mumbai taxpayers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a Section 143(1) intimation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An intimation (not scrutiny) from CPC Bengaluru after processing your ITR. It shows income as computed by CPC, tax payable or refund, and any adjustments (TDS corrections, deduction disallowances, AIS income additions). If demand is raised, pay within 30 days or file Section 154 rectification if the adjustment is incorrect. Patron analyses 143(1) intimations and files rectification for incorrect demands."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is Section 148 reassessment?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Issued when the AO believes income has escaped assessment. This is a serious notice. Before issuing 148, the AO sends a Section 148A show-cause notice giving 30 days to explain. If not satisfied, Section 148 is issued requiring a return. Covers 3 years (normal) or 10 years (escaped income > Rs 50 lakh or foreign assets). For Mumbai property sellers and high-value transaction individuals, 148 is the most consequential notice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the penalty for not responding to an IT notice?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Non-response to 142(1): Rs 10,000 penalty under Section 271(1)(b) plus possible search warrant. Non-response to 143(2): best judgement assessment under Section 144 (AO estimates income, typically unfavourable). Non-correction of 139(9) within 15 days: ITR invalidated. Non-payment of 156 demand within 30 days: recovery proceedings (bank attachment). Under-reporting: 50% penalty; misreporting: 200% under Section 270A."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I file rectification for an incorrect CPC demand?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. If the 143(1) intimation has errors (wrong TDS credit, incorrect deduction disallowance, computational mistakes), file Section 154 rectification on the e-filing portal. CPC processes rectification in 30-60 days with revised intimation. Common CPC errors for Mumbai: ignoring TDS from job changes, missed HRA exemption, incorrect loss set-off. Patron files rectifications and tracks resolution."
          }
        },
